Notice that the lower picture shows that the three pipes fit inside the equilateral triangle with side length 60.  However, there must be some space above the vertical grid lines.
To find the space, we will use a graph with the unit of measurement in the $y-$axis equal to the radius of the circles, and the $x-$axis split so that the centers of the bottom two pipes from the lower picture have coordinates (0,0) and (60,0).  I will call the circle with center at (0,1) as $A,$ and the circle with center at (60,1) as $B.$
[asy]
fill((0,0)--(60,0)--(30,30)--cycle,black);
fill(circle((0,1),1),white);
fill(circle((60,1),1),white);
draw((0,0)--(60,0)--(30,30)--cycle);
dot((0,1));
dot((60,1));
label("$A$",(-.75,.77));
label("$B$", (59.25, 0.77));
draw((6,0)--(6,1)--(0,1),EndArrow(size=5));
draw((6,0)--(0,0),EndArrow(size=5));
draw((59.05,30)--(60,30)--(60,1),EndArrow(size=5));
